
Variables
=========

Variables  are  used  to  store values.  They are specified by the '$'
(dollar)  sign, followed by a series of alphanumeric characters.  When
you  execute  a command the values of the variables are subsituted for
the  variables, before the command is actually executed.  If you don't
wish  to  have  $  treated  as  a variable introducer, you may want it
treated  literally by putting a '\' infront of it.  For example, /ECHO
$time will display the time, but /ECHO \$time will display "$time".

There  are  two  types of variables, runtime and environment.  Runtime
variables  are  temporary  and  are  usually  updated  as commands are
executed,  but is settable by the /SET command.  Environment variables
are   used  to  configure  the  user's  environment.   Unlike  runtime
variables,  environment variables are not changed by the system.  They
can  be  permanent  if you save them by /SETENV SAVE.  These variables
are changed by the /SETENV command.

